{"attack": true, "case": "The target's 'last man standing' mechanism presupposes that the process of elimination converges on a finite survivor, but the target's OWN countercondition admits the fatal case: 'If the set of possible theories is infinite, meaning elimination never leaves a \"best\" candidate.' This condition is not hypothetical — it is actual. The space of possible theories is demonstrably infinite: any refuted theory can be salvaged by an ad hoc adjustment, and infinitely many unconceived, unrefuted theories always coexist with the currently preferred one. Consequently, there is never a 'last man standing'; there are always infinitely many theories standing. The entire evolutionary-elimination metaphor ('we have no other candidates left standing,' 'the others are dead') collapses, because the others are NOT dead — infinitely many survive untested. Once the pool is infinite, mere non-elimination cannot generate a preference; some additional criterion (degree of corroboration, severity of test) must rank the survivors, and that ranking criterion is precisely what the induction charge targets. The target thus fails its own stated countercondition and the metaphor it relies upon is mechanistically inapplicable.", "premise": "The set of possible scientific theories is infinite, so elimination through refutation never reduces the candidate pool to a single or finite 'best' survivor.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "8cbcaa2c2d1c917", "quote": "If the set of possible theories is infinite, meaning elimination never leaves a 'best' candidate."}], "counterexample": ["Consider two theories, T1 (currently best-corroborated) and T2 (an unrefuted variant of T1 with an ad hoc clause that makes no new observable predictions). Both 'survive' elimination. The target's 'last man standing' rule provides no basis to prefer T1 over T2, since neither is 'dead.' Yet scientists rationally prefer T1. This preference cannot be explained by elimination alone and requires a corroboration-severity judgment — reintroducing the very inductive element the target claims is a category error."], "successor_question": "If the set of unrefuted theories is always infinite, what non-inductive criterion does the target propose to rank among the survivors, given that mere survival provides no such ranking?"}